Date: Wed, 29 Sep 93 1:34:00 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Private Dossier Service Now Available

Dossiers and Reputation Markets

Announcing the Launch of a Prototype Dossier Service


I am compiling dossiers on the postings of Extropians on this list,
and have been for some time. I have of course referred to my archives,
my databases, etc., several times over the past 15 months, so this
should not be of any surprise to readers of the List.

While Extropians List rules forbid me from forwarding posts in toto to
people not on the list at the time the posts originally appeared, the
practice of quoting such posts is clearly allowed (as new people are
always being added, and quotes of past messages are clearly allowed).

Facts, quotes, political opinions, personal anecdotes, etc., are thus
freely accessible to anyone who bothers to archive and compile these
factoids. Photographs of approximately 50 List current or past list
members have been put into the database by Video Spigot digitizing of
Hi-8 tapes made at various Extropian functions.

Anyway, I use a combination of Double Helix, a relational database
with excellent text attachment (BLOB technology) capabilities, and
Thought Pattern, a free-form text manager, for these files. The files
are stored on several 128 MB magneto-optical cartridges.

I am just about ready to launch my service, a private dossier access
service. The uses are manifold.

Access to the system will be denominated either in dollars, with a
credit line established (to avoid the transfer of checks for trivial
amounts), or in Thornes, with a rate set by me. Probably this Thorne
rate will be based roughly on what I think the conversion rate from
Thornes to dollars is--more on this later.

I also need more information to add to my dossiers. I am willing to
pay for information of all sorts, but of course the rate will have to
be negotiated, will depend on the "juiciness" of the info, and will be
affected by the reputation of the person selling the information. For
example, someone with juicy info about Perry Metzger's grade point
averages at Columbia might be paid 500 Thornes, while someone else
with reliable info about <deleted>'s bra size might be paid 1000 or
more Thornes.  Wrong or flaky information would have an effect on the
next transaction price, of course.

What type of information is needed? Offer what you think would sell to
others, and see what actually is for sale. The market will evolve.

Some details:

* A larger version of this system, the "offshore data haven" I have
talked about for years (and Bruce Sterling did too, in print), is
possible, using the basic structure I'm using here. I'll leave it
vague for now, for legal reasons. Suffice it to say that an offshore
system would use encryption, would exist offshore to halt parochial
ideas about "privacy," and so on. (The astute amongst you know that
"cypherspace" is about as far offshore as you can get, so the physical
Caymans is not necessarily any better than the digital Caymans.)

(Yes, I'm a privacy advocate, of sorts. But information cannot be
bottled up, and "privacy" also means we have the "right" to compile
diaries, files, dossiers, etc., as we see fit. So long as no coercion
is involved--as would be the case with bans on locks or
encryption--then the individual must protect his or her privacy.)

* I can operate in the open on this list because of the limited-scale
nature of the experiment. Instead of a full-scale "private eye"
dossier system, or a "no bullshit" credit rating agency (the "no
bullshit" meaning that there are no silly mandated rules about how
many years back the records can be kept), the system here will not run
afoul of the legal system...the system here will avoid touchy things
like financial records, medical records (unless in the form of useful
gossip), etc.

* To allow pseudonyms, remailers and encrypted messages can be used.
My PGP public key is included at the end of this announcement.

I can't give a tutorial here, but the idea is simple. The recent
postings on "BlackNet" explained the idea. Pseudonyms allow
"reputation" to build up, as assertions are checked and confirmed (or
not falsified, for you Popperians). Thus, "BozoBob" could digitally
sign his message, which reveals that Pandit Singh failed an AIDS test.
(The sort of scoop probably banned by current law but will flourish
someday in cypherspace.) If confirmed, BozoBob would gain a greater
reputation.

(Not via HEx, but based on my own personal evaluation, eventually
augmented by more automated methods.)

Using HEx and Thornes. 

The HEx system is fairly flawed. For example, the new reputation KLAUS
began trading two nights ago, got some respectable sales, but was
"rep-bombed" by someone yesterday and today, with this curious
result, from the 9-29-93 Nightly Market Report:

                        Market Value

KLAUS (Tim May)            330.04

HAM (Tony Hamilton)    158,180.00

TCMAY (owned by JPP)        69.00

(I'm not making these up. The most recent report shows this. Now, are
the posts of Tony Hamilton, wonderful and profound as they are, really
2200 times as worthy as mine are? Or 500 times as "reputed" as those
of the redoubtable rep of Klaus! von Future Prime? Draw your own
conclusions.)

So, the value of the Thorne is problematic. I will take the bull by
the thornes:

- I will pay $100.00 in personal checks in FRS Notes (dollars,
putatively) for 100,000 Thornes.

- The minimum check size I am interested in writing is $10.00. So, if
you don't have p10,000 or more, get together with your friends, use
the "Transfer" command to pool some Thornes, and then make the offer
to me.

- I will send the check once the Thornes have entered my account
(KLAUS, not TCMAY). 

- If you do not trust me, an escrow service may be a good
idea...anyone out there interested in this? (But do we trust the
"escrow service" not to simply keep the $100?)

The "real value" of Thornes is whatever people will pay for them, in
whatever currencies. The figure of 100 Thornes to the dollar is too
bizarre to discuss (it implies that ever participant in HEx gets $100
just for joining). My figure of 1000 Thornes to the dollar may also be
screwy, but at least it's a start.

Naturally, I assume Rowan is not planning to devalue the Thorne by
printing more...if he does, I lose my investement and will pursue
other remedies (cf. the discussion on gun-toting Nazis).

With 100,000 Thornes, plus what I already have in my KLAUS account (a
whole lot more than the p330 "market value" listed in the nightly
report, obviously), I can use this to buy more stuff for my dossiers.

The way it'll work is that you offer me something juicy about the
personal lifestyles and habits of List members, and I'll tell you how
many Thornes I'll pay for it.

I'll also accept payments in Thornes from List members who don't want
parts of their dossiers sold. Of course, they'll have to pay to see
their dossiers in the first place, then come to an agreement on price
with me....

What an exciting world we're entering.

And here on this List we can experiment with these Crypto Anarchy
structures, even if the software infrastructure for large-scale use is
several years away.

Let's get started.

--Tim May, Owner, BlackNet Investigations

