Also, decide on the gun's mission before you narrow it down. If it's
to be effective as a PPD (personal protection device) it needs to be:

Reliable, Concealable (light and small), Fast (smooth to draw and
shoot, fits your hand), Powerful (viz. the endless "stopping power"
debates on rec.guns).

I agree with TCM that a compact Glock in 9mm is a great compromise of
all of the above. I have shot Glocks and a few other pistols of that
type and the two that really did the job well were Glock (17/19) and
Taurus (PT 92/99).

Glocks are near the high end in price, though, and just a little on
the big side (relative of course), although I have heard rumor of a
"Baby Glock" in 9mm Kurz (.380 ACP) due out around the end of '93.

I personally have my eye on a KBI SMK-380 or the smaller but pricier
SMC-380. The SMK is about $200, is a Walther clone, will supposedly
handle Cor Bon, Hydra-Shok, or other defense loads well, and comes in
the Craig sept's martial colors of black and silver. Both guns fit my
hand well right out of the box. People whose opinion I value identify
.380 as the lower boundary of an effective defense gun, and then only
with the hot loads. If you have a .22 or a .25 pistol, it may be more
effective to throw the gun hard at the aggressor's head than to piss
him off 40 or 45 grains at a time. OTOH, if someone had a .22 aimed
convincingly at my head from a foot away, and gave me a choice
between lying down to wait for the police and seeing how much damage
the gun would do, the choice would be a ... no-brainer.

