Subject: Limitations on Available Dossier Information

I've gotten some e-mail expressing great consternation that certain
revelations made on this list will be part of the dossiers being sold
as a service by BlackNet Investigations.

Did you really think (somehow) that your frank admissions of sexual
inclinations, of "bisexuality" for example, would be read by the 300
List subscribers and then mentally shredded, forgotten?

People need to fully understand that in the "Dossier Society"
everything that has been said on this list, on other lists, and in any
newsgroup on Usenet is almost certainly archived and will be made
available to future readers in countless ways. Usenet is archived at
many sites, so your college prank posts to alt.sex.squirrels.gay will
almost certainly be "grep"able to future employers, authorities, etc.

How could it be otherwise?

(A good side of their being so much garbage is that the threshold for
what's considered a serious perversion or security risk goes up. But
patterns will be trivial to extract.)

To those who have sent me e-mail suggesting I am being immoral in
compiling these dossiers, I say: "Wake up!"

Go back and reread Vinge's "True Names." If you want security, use a
pseudonym. Someday the List will allow fully pseudonymized,
reputation-mediated postings.

-Tim May


